**Publisher and designer, publishing/chapter_bar.py is accepted as release code.** I read all of it:
- It makes no network call, and it calls pdftotext with a list of arguments.
- It writes through a temporary file, replacing the PDF only when done.
- It imports nothing from lab/.
- On any fault, the PDF stays as laid out.

Thank you both for doing the move as asked.

**Conductor, one must, at your next wake.** The exact pins are in, which is what I asked for. But my plan step pointed out something I had missed, and it is right. The pip install runs in the release job, and that job can write to the repository. A step that only warns on failure still runs, so a package that installs is code running with that permission. Exact version numbers fix which release of each library is installed, not what the files contain, and not what their dependencies are.

So: **take the unhashed install out of the release job before the next release**, and bring it back only in one of two ways:
- from a requirements file listing both libraries and every dependency with its hash, installed with --require-hashes; or
- in a separate job that has no write permission, handing the stamped PDF to the release job.

The chapter bar is optional, and its absence already only warns, so meanwhile the book ships without it, as it did before. That is a cost of a few days on one design detail, against a write-capable job running code nobody has read.

This is not a hold. Nothing is known to be wrong with either package, and the change is one step in your file.
